planets = [
    {"title": "Mercury", "color": "Grey", "second_name": "First planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 155, "img": "mercury.jpg", "details": "Mercury is the smallest planet in our solar system and the one closest to the Sun. Because it lacks a substantial atmosphere to trap heat, it experiences the most extreme temperature swings of any planet, ranging from boiling hot during the day to freezing cold at night. Its surface is covered in thousands of impact craters, making it look very similar to our Moon. Despite being so close to the Sun, it is actually not the hottest planet; that title belongs to Venus due to its thick atmosphere. Mercury is a rocky, terrestrial world with a massive iron core that takes up most of its interior. It zips around the Sun faster than any other planet, completing a full orbit in just 88 Earth days. It is categorized as the 'First planet from the sun' to highlight its primary location."},
    {"title": "Venus", "color": "Orange", "second_name": "Second planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 41, "img": "venus.jpg", "details": "Venus is the second planet from the Sun and is often called Earth’s sister planet because they are similar in size and structure. However, Venus is a world of extremes with a thick, toxic atmosphere made mostly of carbon dioxide that traps heat in a runaway greenhouse effect. This makes it the hottest planet in our solar system, with surface temperatures high enough to melt lead. Underneath its yellow-white clouds of sulfuric acid, the surface is a landscape of mountains and thousands of active volcanoes. Unlike most other planets, Venus rotates on its axis backwards, meaning the Sun rises in the west and sets in the east. It is also the brightest natural object in Earth's night sky after the Moon, which is why it is often called the 'Morning Star' or 'Evening Star.' It is described as a terrestrial planet that demonstrates the power of atmospheric pressure and heat."},
    {"title": "Earth", "color": "Blue and green", "second_name": "Third planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 0, "img": "earth.jpg", "details": "Earth is the third planet from the Sun and the only place in the universe known to harbor life. It is the largest of the four terrestrial planets, with a surface covered by approximately 71% liquid water. The atmosphere is composed primarily of nitrogen and oxygen, which protects the surface from harmful solar radiation while maintaining a stable temperature. Earth has a powerful magnetic field generated by its active iron core, which shields the planet from solar winds. It is the only planet in our solar system with active plate tectonics that constantly reshape the landscape through volcanic and seismic activity. The planet has one natural satellite, the Moon, which stabilizes Earth's axial tilt and creates the ocean tides. This unique combination of water, atmosphere, and geological activity makes it a vibrant and habitable world."},
    {"title": "Mars", "color": "Red", "second_name": "Fourth planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 225, "img": "mars.jpg", "details": "Mars is the fourth planet from the Sun and is often called the 'Red Planet' due to the iron oxide, or rust, on its surface. It is a cold, desert world with a thin atmosphere composed mostly of carbon dioxide. Despite its dry appearance, Mars is home to the largest volcano in the solar system, Olympus Mons, and a canyon system called Valles Marineris that is as wide as the United States. Evidence from robotic missions suggests that liquid water once flowed across the surface billions of years ago. Today, water exists mainly as ice in the polar caps and just beneath the Martian soil. The planet has two small moons, Phobos and Deimos, which are thought to be captured asteroids. Because its day length and seasonal tilt are similar to Earth's, it is the most explored planet by rovers searching for signs of ancient life."},
    {"title": "Jupiter", "color": "White and orange", "second_name": "Fifth planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 714, "img": "jupiter.png", "details": "Jupiter is the fifth planet from the Sun and by far the largest planet in our solar system, containing more than twice the mass of all the other planets combined. It is a gas giant primarily composed of hydrogen and helium, much like a star, though it never gained enough mass to ignite. The planet's outer atmosphere is divided into colorful latitudinal bands and dark belts caused by intense storms and jet streams. Its most famous feature is the Great Red Spot, a giant storm larger than Earth that has been raging for hundreds of years. Jupiter has a very powerful magnetic field and at least 95 moons, including the four large Galilean moons: Io, Europa, Ganymede, and Callisto. Despite its massive size, Jupiter is the fastest-spinning planet in the solar system, completing one rotation in less than 10 hours. This rapid rotation flattens the planet slightly at the poles and bulges it at the equator."},
    {"title": "Saturn", "color": "Beige", "second_name": "Sixth planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 1400, "img": "saturn.jpg", "details": "Saturn is the sixth planet from the Sun and the second-largest planet in our solar system, famous for its complex and dazzling system of rings. Like Jupiter, it is a gas giant composed mostly of hydrogen and helium and lacks a solid surface. The rings are not solid objects but are made of billions of individual chunks of ice and rock, ranging in size from tiny dust grains to massive boulders. Saturn is the least dense of all the planets; its density is so low that it would actually float if placed in a giant pool of water. The planet's atmosphere is marked by faint stripes and powerful winds that can reach speeds of 1,800 kilometers per hour. It also hosts a vast family of moons, including Titan, which is the only moon in the solar system known to have a thick atmosphere and liquid lakes on its surface. Despite its massive volume, Saturn rotates very quickly, completing a full day in just about 10.7 hours."},
    {"title": "Uranus", "color": "Cyan", "second_name": "Seventh planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 2900, "img": "uranus.jpg", "details": "Uranus is the seventh planet from the Sun and is classified as an ice giant because most of its mass is a hot, dense fluid of 'icy' materials like water, methane, and ammonia. It has a unique blue-green color caused by methane gas in its atmosphere, which absorbs red light and reflects blue. Unlike any other planet in our solar system, Uranus rotates on its side, making it appear to roll around the Sun like a ball. This extreme tilt is likely the result of a massive collision with an Earth-sized object long ago, causing 21-year-long seasons. It is the coldest planet in the solar system, even though it is not the farthest from the Sun, due to its very low internal heat. Uranus also possesses a faint system of 13 narrow rings and 28 known moons named after characters from the works of William Shakespeare and Alexander Pope. Its atmosphere is dominated by hydrogen and helium but contains more methane than its neighbor, Jupiter or Saturn."},
    {"title": "Neptune", "color": "Blue", "second_name": "Eight planet from the sun", "distance_million_km": 4500, "img": "neptune.jpg", "details": "Neptune is the eighth and most distant major planet from the Sun, orbiting in the cold, dark outer reaches of the solar system. It is the second ice giant, composed mostly of a thick soup of water, ammonia, and methane over a solid, Earth-sized inner core. The methane in its upper atmosphere gives the planet a vivid, bright blue color that is much deeper than the pale cyan of Uranus. Despite its great distance from the Sun, Neptune has an incredibly active atmosphere with the fastest winds ever recorded in the solar system, reaching speeds of over 2,000 kilometers per hour. One of its most famous features was the Great Dark Spot, a massive storm system similar to Jupiter's Great Red Spot, though it eventually disappeared and was replaced by other storms. Neptune has 16 known moons, the largest of which is Triton, a frozen world that orbits the planet in the opposite direction of Neptune’s rotation. Because it is so far away, it is the only planet in our solar system not visible to the naked eye and was the first to be predicted by mathematical calculations before it was actually seen through a telescope."},
    {"title": "Pluto", "color": "Grey and beige", "second_name": "The dwarf 'planet'", "distance_million_km": 5900, "img": "pluto.jpg", "details": "Pluto is a dwarf planet located in the Kuiper Belt, a region of icy objects beyond the orbit of Neptune. It was considered the ninth planet in our solar system from its discovery in 1930 until it was reclassified in 2006. Pluto has a complex surface featuring vast mountains made of water ice and a large, heart-shaped glacier composed of nitrogen and carbon monoxide. Its thin atmosphere is made primarily of nitrogen, which expands when Pluto is closer to the Sun and collapses as it moves farther away. Pluto has five known moons, the largest of which is Charon, which is so big that the two bodies actually orbit each other like a double planet system. The dwarf planet follows a highly elliptical and tilted orbit, which occasionally brings it closer to the Sun than Neptune. Because of its extreme distance, it remains one of the coldest places in the solar system, with temperatures dropping as low as -232°C."},
    {"title": "Sun", "color": "Yellow and orange", "second_name": "Light", "distance_million_km": 149, "img": "sun.jpg", "details": "The Sun is a nearly perfect sphere of hot plasma at the center of our solar system, accounting for about 99.8% of its total mass. It is a yellow dwarf star primarily composed of hydrogen and helium, generating energy through nuclear fusion in its core where temperatures reach 15 million degrees Celsius. This energy radiates outward, providing the light and heat necessary to support life on Earth and driving our planet's weather and climate. The Sun’s surface, or photosphere, is marked by dark sunspots and powerful solar flares that can release massive bursts of energy into space. Its gravity is what holds the entire solar system together, keeping everything from the largest planets to the smallest debris in orbit. The Sun is currently about 4.6 billion years old and is roughly halfway through its life cycle as a main-sequence star. Without its constant energy and gravitational pull, the solar system would not exist as we know it."},
    {"title": "Moon", "color": "Dark grey", "second_name": "Earth's natural satellite", "distance_million_km": 0.4, "img": "moon.jpg", "details": "The Moon is Earth’s only natural satellite and the fifth-largest moon in the solar system. It was likely formed about 4.5 billion years ago when a Mars-sized body collided with the early Earth, sending debris into orbit that eventually coalesced. The Moon is tidally locked to our planet, meaning it rotates at the same rate it orbits, which is why we always see the same side from the surface of Earth. Its landscape is a rugged mix of ancient, cratered highlands and large, dark, flat plains called maria, which were formed by prehistoric volcanic eruptions. The Moon lacks a significant atmosphere, resulting in extreme temperature variations and a sky that remains black even during the day. Its gravitational pull is responsible for the rising and falling of Earth's ocean tides and helps stabilize our planet's axial tilt. As the brightest and largest object in our night sky, the Moon remains the only celestial body beyond Earth that humans have physically visited."},
    {"title": "Black hole", "color": "Black", "second_name": "Infinite gravity", "distance_million_km": 15000000000, "img": "blackhole.jpg", "details": "A black hole is a region of space where gravity is so intense that nothing, not even light, can escape from it. This happens because matter has been squeezed into an incredibly tiny space, usually occurring at the end of a massive star's life cycle. The boundary surrounding the black hole is called the event horizon, often referred to as the 'point of no return.' Once something crosses this threshold, the gravitational pull is so strong that escape becomes mathematically impossible. At the very center of a black hole lies the singularity, a point of near-infinite density where the known laws of physics cease to function. Despite their name, black holes are not empty holes but are instead packed with a tremendous amount of mass. Most galaxies, including our own Milky Way, are thought to have supermassive black holes at their centers that contain the mass of millions or even billions of suns. Because they emit no light, they are invisible to the naked eye and can only be detected by observing how their gravity affects nearby stars and gas."},
]

